-        <p>Suppose \(f\) is twice continuously differentiable, \(\hat{x}(a_0)\) is a point where \(f_x = 0\) and \(f_{xx} \ne 0\), and \(\hat{x}\) is continuous at \(a_0\). Apply Theorem 1.3 of Lesson L21, assuming its Assumption 1.2 (the intermediate value theorem) and Assumption 2.1 of Lesson L06 (the extreme value theorem), to \(G(a, x) = f_x(x, a)\), with \(a\) as the independent variable and \(x\) as the dependent one, at \((a_0, \hat{x}(a_0))\), where the partial derivative of \(G\) in \(x\) is \(f_{xx} \ne 0\). The theorem gives a differentiable function \(\tilde{x}\) on an interval \(I\) around \(a_0\), with values in an interval \(J\) around \(\hat{x}(a_0)\), and \(f_x(\tilde{x}(a), a) = 0\). For \(a\) near \(a_0\), \(\hat{x}(a)\) lies in \(J\) by continuity, and \(f_x(\hat{x}(a), a) = 0\) by Lemma 1.2. The zero of \(f_x(\cdot, a)\) in \(J\) is unique, so \(\hat{x}(a) = \tilde x(a)\) there, which gives (H3) locally.</p>
