             <span class="problem-title">E3 · The price in cents (Lab 1)</span>
             <p>Lab 1 prints, at the yield 0.05 (the base yield plus the shift 0.01), a price of 100000 cents and the second-order expansion there rounded to 100015 cents. Explain why the expansion is rounded to an integer for the comparison, and state which quantity is money.</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
-              <p>The price is money, and money is reported as an integer number of cents. The lab's price line is the unrounded price rounded to the cent. Proposition 2.2 gives exactly face at the yield 5 percent; the float yield the lab uses differs from 5 percent by about 2.8e-18, which moves the unrounded price by about 2.1e-12 cents, so the rounded price is 100000. The lab prints neither the offset of 2.8e-18 nor its effect on the unrounded price, which is about 2.1e-12 cents. The expansion is an approximation of that money amount, and it is rounded to the nearest cent so that the printed comparison is in whole cents. The derivatives are sensitivities in cents per unit of yield, and the lab keeps them as floats, each rounded once to the nearest double. The price itself is an exact rational, so the money amount contains no float; the expansion is built from the float sensitivities.</p>
